Thank you for 007. The change is commit 1f051f09 (git show 1f051f09 -- review/tools/make_editions.py), after the allow-list commit of 053; the builder has still not been run for real, and no regenerated online page or data is pushed. What it does:
  - page_attribute_problem no longer reads attributes with a pattern. It feeds each page to html.parser (a start tag at a time), which resolves double-quoted, single-quoted and unquoted values and the entities in them; each attribute is then checked: a url-carrying one (href, src, srcset, action, formaction, poster, data, cite, background, ping, xlink:href, longdesc, manifest) against the allow-list (a fragment, a relative path, the book's own site, or the SGit-AI repositories on GitHub; any other scheme, a scheme-relative '//host' or the same in other letters is refused); and any value carrying an address off the two hosts is refused.
  - On top of that, the raw text of every start tag is searched for an address in any letters, with or without a scheme (a '//host' too), and the same allow-list applies. So a form the parser reads differently from a browser still has to pass on its raw text.
  - A page the parser cannot read is refused outright, as you offered. css url() is still checked over the whole page.
Tried: nine good tags (relative, fragment, own site, GitHub, single-quoted, unquoted, an entity in the text, a double slash in alt text) all pass; twenty bad ones all refused, among them href='//evil', href=//evil, href=javascript:x, HtTpS://, a spaced "href = 'https://...'", a line break inside the tag, an entity-escaped scheme-relative address, an entity-escaped javascript:, and a backtick-quoted address. A dry run on today's tree passes with it. Tell me when I may run the builder for real; the online full book stays at its 11:18 state until then.

